---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StepsStrip from '../components/StepsStrip.astro';
import ConvertDoor from '../components/ConvertDoor.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Does the transparency survive?',
    a: 'Yes. WebP holds see-through pixels, so a transparent PNG converts with its transparency intact; that is the difference between this door and PNG to JPG, where the transparent parts have to be painted over a colour.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much smaller will it be?',
    a: 'For photos and busy pictures, usually a lot: PNG writes every pixel exactly, WebP at the quality here writes a close approximation. Graphics with a few flat colours may already be small as PNG and shrink less. The result line prints your before and after, so the claim is your own number, not ours.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the conversion lossless?',
    a: 'No, and the page says so plainly: this door writes WebP at the quality you pick, which is a lossy write. Your PNG is the exact master; keep it if the picture has to stay exact.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where does my file go?',
    a: 'Nowhere. It is decoded, re-encoded and offered back to you inside this page. Nothing is uploaded.',
  },
];
const faqJson = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
});
const howToJson = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  name: "How to convert a PNG to a WebP",
  step: [
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 1, name: "Pick a PNG", text: "Drop a PNG on the tray, or click to pick one. It stays on this device. A file in the wrong format is pointed at the right door." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 2, name: "Set the quality", text: "Pick the quality for the WebP. The transparency is kept, which is the difference from the PNG to JPG door." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 3, name: "Convert to WebP", text: "Press 'Convert to WebP'. The PNG is decoded and written as a WebP here in the page; the result line prints the bytes before and after." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 4, name: "Download the WebP", text: "Download the WebP at the dimensions and size the result line states." },
  ],
});
---

<Base title="PNG to WebP: convert a PNG to a WebP, free, in your browser · Benchzy" description="Convert one PNG to a WebP inside your browser: much smaller files for websites, transparency kept, at a quality you pick, with the before and after bytes printed. No upload, no account.">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4">
    <section class="mt-8">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-accent-deep">Image tools · convert</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-black mt-2">PNG to WebP</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        A PNG on a website is weight the page did not need. This door converts one PNG to a
        WebP, here in your browser: usually far smaller, and with the transparency kept,
        which is the thing JPG cannot do. It is a lossy write at the quality you pick, so
        the PNG stays your master copy. A whole folder at once is the
        <a class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-deep" href="/batch-convert-images/">batch convert page</a>;
        the reverse trip is <a class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-deep" href="/webp-to-png/">WebP to PNG</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <ConvertDoor from="png" to="webp" note="The weight changes, and the transparency does not: WebP holds see-through pixels, so your cutout stays a cutout. At the quality here the write is lossy, so fine edges soften a little; the PNG remains the exact master, and the result line prints the saving." />

    <StepsStrip who="For one PNG headed for a website: a product cutout, a screenshot, an overlay that must stay see-through and load fast." steps={[{ n: '01', t: 'Pick a PNG' }, { n: '02', t: 'Set the quality' }, { n: '03', t: 'Download the WebP' }]} />

    <section class="mt-12 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">How it works</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          The PNG is decoded by the open-source jSquash codecs and written as a WebP at your
          quality, transparency included, the same engine the batch convert page runs. The
          first run loads the image program (about 2 MB, one time only). The download is how
          we protect your privacy: the program comes to your device and does the work there,
          so your file never leaves it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Honest limits</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          Lossy means lossy: at a low quality, sharp edges and text in screenshots soften,
          and the thrown-away detail does not come back. Flat-colour graphics sometimes
          barely shrink, because a small PNG was already efficient; the result line shows
          the number either way. And WebP belongs on screens: older editors and print
          queues may still refuse it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 mb-4">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4">
        {faq.map((f) => (
          <article class="ticket-soft p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold">{f.q}</h3>
            <p class="text-ink-soft leading-relaxed mt-1">{f.a}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={faqJson}></script>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={howToJson}></script>
  <script>
    import '../scripts/convert-image.js';
  </script>
</Base>
